course: dictionary
lesson: 16121
title: Old words, same meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Some words belong to old books, prayers and Bible readings. People rarely say them today. Wheresoever is one of these old-fashioned, formal words. It means wherever, in any place.
explain: Use the context clues to find the meaning. «Wheresoever you go, I will follow you.» The words you go and I will follow tell you that wheresoever means in any place you go.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping in a modern word. «Wherever you go, I will follow you.» It still makes sense, so wheresoever must mean wherever.
fact: Wheresoever means wherever, in any place.
fact: It is old-fashioned and formal, so you will mostly meet it in old books, prayers and Bible readings.
fact: Today people usually say «wherever» instead.
fact: Wheresoever can work as an adverb, and in old-fashioned writing it can also be a conjunction, a joining word.
fact: A common phrase is «wheresoever you go», which means in any place you go.
exercise: «Wheresoever you hide, I will find you.» What does wheresoever mean? | Only once | *In any place | Very quickly | Never
exercise: Which modern word means the same as wheresoever? | Whenever | Whatever | Whichever | *Wherever
exercise: Where would you most likely meet the word wheresoever? | *In an old book or a prayer | In a text message | On a football shirt | In a weather report
test: «Wheresoever the king travelled, his guards went too.» What does wheresoever mean here? | Because the king was tired | *In any place the king went to | Before the king left | Only in the castle
test: Which is the modern word for wheresoever? | Whichever | *Wherever | Whoever | Whenever
exam: «Wheresoever you go, carry this light.» What is the best meaning? | Carefully, one step at a time | Only at night | Whenever the light goes out | *Wherever you go, in any place
